Oh my RNG, after ~3 years playing this game I finally ascended! I
didn't
take notes during the game, but I ttyrec'd it, so I'm going to play it
back now and narrate the saga of DoubleF, an
        artifact-wishless
        (mostly) colourless (black and white ASCII)
        polyless
        genoless
        polyselfless
        genoselfless (aka managed not to get himself killed ;) )
neutral human wizard...

The most interesting part is mostly the beginning, so it's longer.

On DL1, he found a glass orb. It was somewhat hefty, but he decided to
keep it as if he knew he would need it in the future... When he had
been
preparing for the journey, a mysterious voice whispered to him: ``Throw
daggers! Throw daggers!''. But he neglected the advice and decided to
fight his way in a wizardly way --- with spells, that is, of which he
knew two: force bolt and magic missile. He, however, collected daggers
just in case. On DL2 he heard someone cursing shoplifters and soon
found
a liquor shop. It sold a large amount of potions, but most of them were
too expensive for him, and the shopkeeper didn't even make a discount
for a compatriot;).

On DL3 he entered the mines. A voice whispered to him: ``Don't enter
the
mines before you can withstand poisonous darts, spikes and arrows!''.
He
again neglected the advice... He went on using spells when something
started attacking him from the dark. There were a lot of easy foes to
kill, so he soon gained enough experience to have significant magic
power to protect himself. He went on collecting daggers, too, but
preferred to practice magic more. He also collected orchish arrows for
their deadly poison. He just stood on the upstairs so that he could
escape to heal and recharge in case something nasty appears.

DoubleF thought that his original kitten wouldn't last too long, and he
didn't even name it. But it soon grew powerful enough to defend him, so
that he didn't have to spend his mana. He didn't find a single lit
level
until Minetown. The priest of Ptah didn't welcome him, but he still
could identify blessings with the altar.

There are always some critical moments in the game; one of them was in
Minetown where ho got surrounded at only 2 HP. He had a scroll of
taming
from his initial inventory, but decided to pray instead. Thoth restored
him to health and he escaped the situation.

On the same level, DoubleF came across a weird creature: a literate
kobold. After killing him, he found out it was carrying several
spellbooks and a couple of scrolls. Later he found out that the kobold
had robbed a book shop. From the spellbooks, he learnt the spells of
light and remove curse; another spellbook blinded him and he had to
flee
from a mob of unknown creatures. He cast magic missile onto them when
something got angry and started hitting him really hard. That was when
his taming scroll came into play. Being blind, he recited the scroll's
runes as he remembered them, and it worked: the angry creature let him
go. A tame wolf was a byproduct of this enchantment.

DoubleF respected Izchak the shopkeeper and carefully closed the door
every time he came to his shop, so that the wolf wouldn't trouble
Izchak
(DoubleF didn't a leash nor a magic whistle). However, Izchak didn't
have a lot to offer: he only had a brass lantern, which he didn't need
after he learnt the spell of light, and candles, of which DoubleF
bought
7.

He soon found another crystal ball, and figured one damn thing is
enough. He used one of them to find traps in the mines, and after it
was
discharged, he threw it into the dungeon wall and watched it shatter
into a thousand of pieces with satisfaction. Still deeper he found a
luckstone, identified by the stupid naming trick, and a cursed lamp. He
put on an uncursed amulet he had bought and found it to be ESP.

His spell powers were limited, and he resorted to daggers. Still,
spells
were a lot better when fighting on an E-square, as they didn't disturb
the E-word, and getting the daggers back did.

An invisible storm giant materialized from nowhere and brought
DoubleF's
HP down to 5. DoubleF retreated and let the pets do the job. He tried
to
tame the giant with another taming scroll, but it failed. But at HP 9,
Pw 3 and AC 9 (he was going armorless to carry more and to cast
better),
his speed system knowledge and pacifist skills of running away and
dancing around the cat and the wolf saved him. He ate the corpse and
became stronger.

On the same level he found a polytrap. The trick was to polymorph the
pets without either a leash or a magic whistle. However, as he was
wearing his trusty CoMR, he could stand on the trap and displace the
pet, which he did. He came up with a purple worm, which was one of his
favorites. Because of the telltale sound, he called him Blurp I,
because
he knew some rotation would intervene sooner or later. The other pet
became a baby long worm, and DoubleF was astonished to learn he
couldn't
displace it! He ditched the useless pet. A succubus helped him gain
some
experience and constitution. He had also advanced to skilled in attack
spells (which was the point of his wizardly approach) and in divination
spells (light), which he knew would be very useful later.

The End of Mines had a wine cellar, where DoubleF found a lot of booze.
Later he found a lamp which was the same sort his was. He wasn't sure
if
it was magic, but he figured it was a very small chance, so he called
it
unmagic. The problem was that he didn't have a sack to put all the loot
from the mines into, and became burdened. As he already had a
luckstone,
he decided not to explore further just yet, and return with Blurp I to
the Minetown to get a discount out of the shopkeepers' throats;). He
stayed polite to Izchak, and Izchak offered him $25 for one of his
lamps! They must be both magic! Moreover, the altar said one of them
was
blessed! He got a blessed +2 SDSM from the lamp, which together with a
Green-Elf's leather hat got his AC to -3.

A stupid thing he did was not being careful enough to spare the priest.
Burrrrp. He got some nice spellbooks, like create monster and detect
unseen. His wish was, of course, to have The Advanced Conjurer's
Trilogy
(B+CrM, B+ChM, B+P) in possession, but there was no rush. He converted
the altar and did some camping, which improved his clerical spells. Not
having a normal bag really sucked. Blurp also ate most of the
sacrifices-in-the-making whole.

As soon as he found a decent small shield and a good dagger, he dropped
the quarterstaff and used those. He found potions of unholy water and
paralysis, which he thought would be handy. Another shop had a
spellbook
of stone to flesh.

He got back into the dungeon and demanded a discount from Klodzko the
liquor emperor. When you have to kill a man, it costs nothing to be
polite (provided you have the zorkmids from the other shopkeepers).
DoubleF paid him for some of his potions before --- burrrrp.
DoubleF and Blurp descended deeper into the main dungeon. He stashed
all
his yet-useless stuff into a large box. At T:12615, already an expert
in
attack spells. He found a pair of levitaion boots by trying them on and
uncursing later with a spell. A level with a sink and an altar was
tempting, but DoubleF didn't want to get bored. Thoth gave him
Magicbane
for a leprechaun corpse.

        The magic-absorbing blade cancels the orc shaman!
        You absorb magical energy!

Who said MB's special cancel attack is useless? Wizard/shaman farming,
anyone?

Quaff-ID'ed B!GA, which helped a lot. The Oracle statues gave a +DF
(crap). A nymph stole the CoMR; daggers. Made some mistakes in Sokoban,
but didn't worry. Found a bug (?): if you teleport a boulder into a
hole
(and don't see it?), the original boulder glyph stays on the screen.
Murphy brought an "oR on the top. Still had a good time whacking at
monsters, not for the amulet, but for the loot (spinach, which brought
him to St:18; some rings).

        The rock mole eats 217 gold pieces!

Don't you just hate it when it happens?

Forgot +FB and +MM with spellbooks deep down in the mines. Daggers,
Magicbane, hack, slash, throw. Finally found them and a bag. It turned
out not to be holding. Wasted the second lamp wish on a blessed
fireproof BoH. Put all those potions from the shop into it.

Don't you think purple worms have special magnetism which attracts
chameleons? Blurp became a balrog! Crap:( At least balrogs don't
interfere with altar camping. Got crowned and given the finger;).
Bless-identified much stuff, found out the scroll of enchant armor,
lowered AC.

Lost Blurp I to a levelport trap. Had to kill him later. Reviewed
Sokoban for extra unnoticed loot. Found a wand of wishing
(engrave-ID'd)! Wished for a blessed magic marker. Found Ludios portal
near a temple. Bought some protection, converted the fort into an
antiquity exhibition with lots of statues (some of them underwater:) ).
Got lots of food and other good stuff.

Got experienced enough to get allowed to quest. It had a lot of tengu,
thus DoubleF got TT and TC. A lemure with an amulet of life saving.
What
a useless life to save...

By the time he got to the Dark One, he forgot +MM again... No, this is
not the last time his memory failed him. He was still at 55% failure
with +FoD --- that means that every time he tried it on the Dark One,
he
failed. Finally DoubleF cancelled the Dark One with Magicbane and
absorbed hsi last magic powers.

Medusa was on an island. Smashing hordes of gremlins with Expert
fireball was pure satisfaction:) Had a risky encounter with a
mindflayer, but the helm and extreme luck saved him.

Wrote the second book from the Trilogy --- +ChM, enchanted more armor,
reorganized the stashes, feeling good enough to do the castle. Buffed
up
strength by eating a giant, ^T until not satiated, eating a giant, etc.
Every giant is sacred:)

A master lich teleported to greet DoubleF. Killed him even though he
was
standing on the stairs. Others: fireball, magic missile. Got the wand,
stashed everything into the central chest. Found [WW.

VotD: Found +DM. Being skilled at divination pays yet again. Found a
bag
of holding. An erynis summoned Yeenoghu. Stabbed him to death with
Magicbane --- just a point of style:)

When DoubleF was leading the wraiths downstairs, he noticed that they
were more eager to follow him if he's visible. So he had to put on a
mummy wrapping (which was dangerous, but worked). Wraiths got him to XL
27.

Asmodeus was annoying. DoubleF found a shirt there and enchanted it.
Not
fireproofing the armor didn't matter much at maxed luck: only the boots
and the gloves got slightly burnt. Juiblex didn't even engulf him.

The next wish went for an uncursed magic marker, as it was important to
be able to write cursed scrolls. DoubleF bought clairvoyance from the
priest and proceeded deeper. Baalzebub was a wimp. Vlad was easy to
find
and a wimp (2xMM). DoubleF tamed a jabberwock and called it, naturally,
Jabberd. Managing it was easier with the magic whistle he had found.
Soon Blurp II joined the company. Orcus was... a demon... a statue...
some rocks... (could have been a demon... a statue... a demon... a
statue... with +S2F).

DoubleF used a technique inspired by the Ghost Busters movie to clean
out Orcustown. He threw his lamp into an unlit location and then his
fireball spells could lock onto the now-lit location. He could have
used
+L, but that would use mana and probably have smaller range.
He also used magic traps for the magical explosions.

Liches and mindflayers weren't all that annoying with Magicbane and
+FoD
respectively. He marked the VS, and went to clear the tower up to
Rodney. His memory failed him again, he forgot +ID and was struggling
to
recall +MM. Rodney's zoo had the same ghost busters technique (of
course, +MM first, to clean the route for the lamp to fly by). On his
way back to relearn the spells, he used his digging wands to make
shortcuts between the stairs. He re-read all the spellbooks which he
had
forgotten. He labelled some of his inventory by the names of the places
he would use them in, because he knew from his previous incarnations
that that reduces the possibility of silly mistakes. He also wrote a
spellbook of cure sickness, fearing the same demonic creature that
killed his previous incarnation, wished for a blessed +3 helm of
brilliance, wished for a magic marker, enchanted armor down to -40.

On the way back, he decided to go straight to the tower bottom with
C?T,
but arrived in the VotD. The priest didn't want to talk to him, as he
didn't have money. The last thing the priest heard was the sound of
DoubleF's magic whistle. Burrrp.

Blurp II ate something bad and became a giant spider. Crap. DoubleF
tamed Blurp III to replace him. Rotation, rotation. Blurp IV joined the
team soon.

The top of Rodney's tower became... sort of Ludios, see above. It also
generated the last giant DoubleF needed to get to 18/**. The foolish
thing DoubleF did was whacking Rodney with the rubber chicken while the
Wizard was levitating over the moat. DoubleF didn't have an amulet of
magical breathing. But he told himself to calm down and zapped the
underwater statue with a wand of teleport. He soon found the statue
still in the tower, but the book he needed wasn't there! He again told
himself to calm down, and took his second (you remember?) crystal ball
out of his BoH (being a packrat pays;)). He used it to search for
spellbooks, and found it. Of course, it took time, and DoubleF had
forgotten the +FoD spell (it hadn't been forgotten when he was
re-reading the spellbooks)! The spellbook was ~20 levels above, and he
didn't want to use his emergency cursed scrolls of teleportation. He
decided to go with his ordinary spells.

A black glow once uncursed his BoH, bringing him to stressed. That's
what the emergency cancellation sack is for, normally, but as the BoH
wasn't cursed, he decided not to use the emergency items and used plain
!HW from the bag to bless it.

He brought only one of the pet purple worms to the sanctum. He was
intending to leave in a hurry anyway. Blurp IV was still chewing on a
tasty (wraith?) corpse when DoubleF, having dropped +BotD to stay
unburdened, quaffed a C!GL to escape the sanctum with the amulet. He
had
killed the priest without getting into the inner sanctum, and gotten
the
``murderer'' message. No worries.

A nasty master mind flayer appeared while DoubleF was still in
Gehennom.
Luckily, it was stupid enough to zap him with a /oS instead of using
the
tentacle attack.

Rodney appeared. 1x+CoC. The mysterious force was rather annoying.
DoubleF relearnt the spells from his stash, dropped some useless stuff
and started the run up from the Castle.

Since then, Rodney had been given the finger. No compromise. Gremlins
on
the Medusa level --- again.

